{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>If a corpse is found in the land, etc.</b> Mahari'a raised a difficulty concerning this pericope: there is no place for it among the four pericopes that speak of the matter of war, and the Rav gave a weak reason for it. Other commentators too press forward to explain the juxtaposition with strained shifts. And now lift up your eyes and see what the Sages said (Sotah 46a): It was taught, Rabbi Yohanan ben Saul said: Why did the Torah say to bring the eglah arufah? The Holy One, blessed be He, said: Let the calf that did not produce fruit come and be decapitated in a place that does not produce fruit, and let it atone for this one whom they did not allow to produce fruit. And the Gemara asks: If you say it refers to procreation, then should one who is a saris and too old not be decapitated? Rather, it refers to mitzvot. And in the pericope preceding this one it is written: ״כִּי מִמֶּנּוּ תֹאכֵל וְאֹתוֹ לֹא תִכְרֹת רַק עֵץ אֲשֶׁר תֵּדַע כִּי לֹא עֵץ מַאֲכָל הוּא״, and the Sages said in tractate Ta'anit (7a): if he is a fit scholar — ״מִמֶּנּוּ תֹאכֵל וְאֹתוֹ לֹא תִכְרֹת״; ״רַק עֵץ אֲשֶׁר תֵּדַע כִּי לֹא עֵץ מַאֲכָל הוּא״ — this is a scholar who is not fit, etc. And if so, every discerning person will see with the eye of his intellect that the juxtaposition of these two pericopes is self-evident, whichever way you turn it: if the reason for the eglah arufah is that they did not allow him to produce fruit in procreation — it is well juxtaposed to the preceding pericope which is zealous about a fruit-bearing tree not to cut it down, and what difference is it to me the fruit of man, what difference is it to me the fruit of the earth — one law applies to them both, as it says: ״כִּי הָאָדָם עֵץ הַשָּׂדֶה״; and if it is that they did not allow him to produce the fruit of mitzvot according to the conclusion — behold, also in the pericope before it there was zeal concerning this, not to cut down the scholar who produces fruit of the tree of life after its kind. And I am astonished at all the commentators that something as plain as this should be hidden from before their eyes." "And as for what the Sages expounded (Ta'anit 7a), the verse ״מִמֶּנּוּ תֹאכֵל וְאֹתוֹ לֹא תִכְרֹת״ concerning the scholar — it appears that they never intended to remove the verse from its plain sense, but rather sought to resolve why the Torah was so insistent about cutting down a food-tree. Surely it was in order to draw from it an a fortiori argument concerning the man who produces the fruit of the righteous, the tree of life, for man is the tree of the field — he produces the fruit of the righteous and the growth of righteousness; and if the Torah was insistent about a fruit-bearing tree that has no knowledge and understanding, all the more so concerning the fruit of the perfect righteous one. And therefore it immediately adjoined the pericope of the eglah arufah, from which there also appears a great insistence upon the fruit of the righteous, the tree of life."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {0 "Mahari'a (מהרי״א): the identity of this commentator is uncertain; the abbreviation could refer to various figures. I have transcribed it as printed."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:13:05.838505882Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}